@page "/sign-in"
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Endpoints
@* A circuit has no response to write Set-Cookie to, and AD-14 turns prerendering off - so this
   page renders statically, which is the branch App.razor already has for the error and not-found
   pages, and HttpContext.SignInAsync is available on the form POST. *@
@attribute [ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]
@inject IUserService Users
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer
@inject NavigationManager Navigation

<PageTitle>@Localizer["SignInTitle"]</PageTitle>

@* The screen's title, in the heading row every other screen carries. The wrapper is written here
   for the same reason it is written on a screen with nothing beside its title: the product has one
   heading shape, and `.dt-page-head h1` zeroes the heading's own margin so `mb-4` decides the gap
   under it rather than the browser.

   The heading is bare, and deliberately so. This screen is a form: the heading names the work, and
   a glyph on it would be decoration beside the boxes that are the actual content. The two status
   pages do carry one in their <h1> - AccessDenied and NotFound - and that is not an inconsistency:
   there the glyph IS the status, the whole of what the page has to report, and it says "refused" or
   "nothing here" before a word is read. A form has no status to report until it is submitted. *@
<div class="dt-page-head mb-4">
    <h1 class="dt-type-h1">@Localizer["SignInTitle"]</h1>
</div>

@* The panel, and the whole of what this screen asks about its own width. The wrapper is this
   screen's element and DtCard's <div> is DtCard's, so the bound is written out here where a scoped
   rule can reach it - a `max-width` aimed at `.dt-card` from this file would carry this component's
   scope attribute onto an element another component rendered and match nothing at all.

   The card itself is titleless. A title would put "sign in" on the page twice, and DtCard's head is
   an <h2> by design - it cannot stand in for the <h1> `FocusOnNavigate` looks for. *@
<div class="dt-sign-in-panel">
    @* Inside the panel, on the same measure as the form it refuses. A banner spanning the whole
       main column above a 30rem card is a sentence whose left edge belongs to one thing and whose
       right edge belongs to another, and the reader has to find the box it is about anyway.

       It keeps what no box below claims - AUTH_INVALID_CREDENTIALS names no field, and saying so
       under one of the two inputs would tell an attacker which one was wrong (FR-4). *@
    <DtFailureBanner Failures="_failures" InlineFields="@InlineFields" />

    <DtCard>
        <EditForm Model="Form!" FormName="signIn" OnValidSubmit="SubmitAsync">
            <div class="mb-4">
                <label class="form-label" for="sign-in-email">@Localizer["Email"]</label>
                @* The tokens a password manager fills these two boxes by. `username` rather than
                   `email` on the identifier: this is the credential half of a sign-in pair, and
                   that is the name the browser stores it under alongside the password. *@
                <InputText id="sign-in-email" class="form-control" autocomplete="username"
                           @bind-Value="Form!.Email" />
                <DtFieldError Field="Email" Failures="_failures" />
            </div>
            <div class="mb-4">
                <label class="form-label" for="sign-in-password">@Localizer["Password"]</label>
                <InputText id="sign-in-password" type="password" class="form-control"
                           autocomplete="current-password" @bind-Value="Form!.Password" />
                <DtFieldError Field="Password" Failures="_failures" />
            </div>

            @* NFR-24: the glyph the navigation gives this destination, beside the word. No
               `disabled` binding, and there is nowhere for one to come from - the page renders
               once per request with no circuit behind it to flip an attribute, and the browser's
               own navigation is what ends the submission. `SubmitGuardTests` records exactly that
               exemption, keyed on `[ExcludeFromInteractiveRouting]` above. *@
            <button class="btn btn-primary dt-sign-in-submit" type="submit">
                <Icon Name="IconName.SignIn" />@Localizer["SignIn"]
            </button>
        </EditForm>
    </DtCard>

    <p class="mt-4">@Localizer["NoAccount"] <a href="/register">@Localizer["Register"]</a></p>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// The boxes this screen renders a <c>DtFieldError</c> for. Stated once and bound by every
    /// banner on the page: the list repeated per banner is exactly the drift FR-83 is broken by,
    /// and a screen carries the banner more than once because a dialog opened with showModal()
    /// leaves the page behind it inert.
    /// </summary>
    private const string InlineFields = "Email,Password";

    [CascadingParameter]
    private HttpContext HttpContext { get; set; } = default!;

    // Nullable with no initializer, because the form binder overwrites the property on a post and
    // an initialized one can be replaced with null (BL0008).
    [SupplyParameterFromForm]
    private SignInInput? Form { get; set; }

    // The property is named Form because the input names the browser posts back are derived from
    // the binding expression - name="Form.Email" - while the form binder looks the model up by this
    // property's name. Different names on the two sides bind nothing, silently.
    protected override void OnInitialized() => Form ??= new SignInInput();

    private IReadOnlyList<ScreenFailure> _failures = [];

    private async Task SubmitAsync()
    {
        try
        {
            var session = await Users.SignInAsync(
                new SignInCommand(Form!.Email, Form.Password),
                HttpContext.RequestAborted);

            // The cookie scheme by name: the default scheme is the path selector, which has nothing
            // of its own to sign in with.
            await HttpContext.SignInAsync(AuthenticationSchemes.Cookie, ClaimsFactory.Build(session));

            Navigation.NavigateTo(LandingRoute.For(session.Role));
        }
        catch (DriveTrackException failure)
        {
            // InputText always renders value="@CurrentValueAsString", so leaving the password on the
            // model would write it back into the HTML of the refusal - into the page cache, the
            // proxy log and the browser's view source.
            Form!.Password = null;

            // The code, resolved through the same catalogue the REST envelope reads, so the same
            // refusal reads the same way in both adapters (NFR-3).
            _failures = FailureKeys.For(failure);
        }
    }

    private sealed class SignInInput
    {
        public string? Email { get; set; }

        public string? Password { get; set; }
    }
}
